  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 138 AM CDT Tue Sep 29 2026\n\nThe NBM has been struggling to keep up with the strong mixing\nacross the Quad State the last few days. Temperatures have been \noverachieving and dewpoints have been lower than forecast,\nresulting in relative humidity in the 20-30% range. With the \nreturn of light southeast winds today, confidence in increasing \ntemperatures is not great enough to alter the NBM forecast, but \nwe did use ConsShort in place of the NBM to lower dewpoints a\nfew degrees through the day. Relative humidity should drop at \nleast to the 25-30% range today, and they could go even lower if\ntemperatures exceed the NBM forecast again. Similar trends are \npossible Wednesday and maybe even Thursday.\n\nGuidance continues to delay the arrival of the cold front and\nonset of precipitation, such that Thursday is now dry, even in\nthe NBM. That will allow temperatures to climb into the lower\n90s throughout the region on Thursday. Outside of forested\nareas, the NBM has a 70-100% chance of reaching 90 or above\nThursday and a 30-90% chance Wednesday. The impact of humidity \nwill be negligible. Even with south winds developing Wednesday \nand becoming gusty on Thursday, dewpoints will be stuck in the \n50s Wednesday and lower 60s Thursday.\n\nGuidance is focusing in on the Friday through Saturday window\nfor our next rain event. The cold front is now expected to push\nthrough the region on Friday, which could allow temperatures\napproach 90 once more over the southern Pennyrile of Kentucky.\nShowers should develop along and behind the front through the\nday and continue across the region Friday night. \n\nA resurgence of showers/rain will spread east across the region\nSaturday, as the remnants of the southern stream system push \neastward across the Quad State. Instability will be very \nlimited, mostly well below 500 J/kg, so thunderstorms are not \nlikely. That said, a few rumbles of thunder cannot be ruled out,\nespecially over the southern half of the Quad State Saturday. \n\nRainfall amounts in this forecast have come down into the 1-2\"\nrange, which is not nearly as extreme as the last few days. The\nNBM forecast still has PoPs running through Sunday and even into\nMonday, so with the expected shorter duration and little to no\nthunder, most places are more likely to see a half inch, with a\nlucky few getting closer to one inch. The 00Z GEFS probability\nof a total of 1\" or more is only 20-50%. \n\nNorthern stream energy will likely push the precipitation out of\nthe Quad State Saturday night, and usher in another shot of\ncooler air. The NBM has highs in the lower half of the 70s and\nlows in the upper 40s and lower 50s Sunday and Monday. It's almost\nas if fall were here.",
